// Storage content — how much raw stock the player may hold, and what
// raises the ceiling (GAMEPLAY.md §5's ingredient cost axis). Data, never
// hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `shop.ron` and `ingredients.ron`; the
// loader is `sim_core::sim::storage::StorageCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. Until it existed, `BuyIngredient` credited
// `OwnedIngredients` with no ceiling at all, so the only limit on stock was
// money — and money is the thing a running factory produces. A player one
// contract into the game could buy four thousand tomatoes and never think
// about supply again, which flattens the "ingredients — recurring, scales
// directly with production volume" cost axis into a single early purchase.
// The cap here is what makes stock a thing you have to build room for.
//
// THE RULE, IN ONE PLACE. Capacity is per ingredient id, and it has two
// parts:
//
//     cap(id) = base_cap + slot_stack * (bays not needed by anything else)
//
// `base_cap` is free and unconditional — every raw can always be held to it
// with no warehouse at all, so a new game works and no ingredient can ever
// be crowded out of existence by another. Above that, stock occupies
// **bays**, and bays are what a warehouse rack supplies.
//
// BAYS ARE DERIVED, NOT ASSIGNED. Nothing stores "bay 3 holds tomato". The
// bays an ingredient occupies are computed from how much of it is held:
//
//     bays_for(count) = ceil(max(0, count - base_cap) / slot_stack)
//
// summed over every ingredient to give bays in use. A purchase is clamped
// so the total never exceeds the bays that exist. This was chosen over
// binding each bay to an id and persisting it: there is no allocation state
// to drift out of step with the ledger, nothing to migrate in a save, and a
// bay frees itself the moment the stock in it is consumed rather than
// staying reserved for an ingredient the player has one unit of.
//
// The consequence to know: free bays are a SHARED pool. Filling every bay
// with tomato does stop the player buying sugar above `base_cap` until some
// tomato is used. That is the intended pressure — a warehouse is a finite
// room — and `base_cap` is the guarantee that it can never become a
// deadlock.
//
// NUMBERS ARE PLACEHOLDERS, like every other price in this repo
// (GAMEPLAY.md §10 puts exact balance in a later pass). The shape they
// encode: `base_cap` is under a hopper's 64-unit capacity, so an
// unwarehoused player is topping bins up by hand and feels it; one rack at
// `slot_stack` x its bays is worth several hoppers of buffer, which is what
// justifies the 75 it costs in `shop.ron` — the same price as a crusher.
(
    schema_version: 1,

    // Free, unconditional capacity per raw ingredient id, with no rack
    // placed anywhere. Deliberately less than one hopper fill: the first
    // thing a player should notice is that they cannot stockpile.
    base_cap: 20,

    // How many units of one ingredient a single bay holds. The last bay an
    // ingredient occupies is usually part full; nothing rounds stock up to
    // a whole bay.
    slot_stack: 50,

    // Every buildable kind that supplies bays, and how many each supplies.
    // A list rather than a single constant so that a larger (or smaller)
    // rack is a data edit and not a Rust one.
    //
    // `warehouse_rack`'s six is the model's own geometry, not a free
    // parameter: the voxel asset is two bays wide by three levels tall
    // (`assets/voxels/generators/assets.rs`, `RACK_POST_X` x
    // `RACK_LEVEL_Z`), and W3 draws one pallet per bay. Changing this
    // number without changing the model leaves the building lying about
    // what it holds.
    racks: [
        RackKind(
            id: "warehouse_rack",
            bays: 6,
        ),
    ],
)
